 ## Proof
 
 
-1.1 Hessenberg reduction uses at most $\max(n-2,0)$ reflector steps. For $n\ge3$, forming one reflector costs $O(n)$ scalar operations, and applying its two-sided similarity to the remaining dense submatrix costs $O(n^2)$; summing over at most $n$ steps gives $O(n^3)$. For $n=1,2$, there are no such steps. [F2, algebra]
+1.1 Hessenberg reduction uses at most $\max(n-2,0)$ reflector steps. For $n\ge3$, forming one reflector costs $O(n)$ scalar operations, and applying its two-sided similarity to the remaining dense submatrix costs $O(n^2)$; summing over at most $n$ steps gives $O(n^3)$. Any full initialization zero-pattern checks also cost at most $O(n^2)$ per stage, hence at most $O(n^3)$ in total. For $n=1,2$, there are no such reflector stages and no below-subdiagonal entries. [F2, algebra]
 
-1.2 For an active block of size $r\ge2$, a Francis sweep has at most $\max(r-2,0)+1$ initial/chase reflectors of length two or three. Each acts on a bounded number of rows and columns across at most $n$ entries. The active block sizes sum to at most $n$, so a full sweep and its non-controller deflation/status work cost $O(n^2)$; for $n=1$ there is no active sweep. The linear sweep budgets therefore give $O(n^3)$ matrix work for the two Schur calls. The same bound covers materializing the recorded factor products: initialization contributes $O(n^3)$ and each bounded-support chase factor updates only a bounded number of dense-factor columns, at cost $O(n)$. [F1, F2, given, algebra]
+1.2 For an active block of size $r\ge2$, a Francis sweep has at most $\max(r-2,0)+1$ initial/chase reflectors of length two or three. Each acts on a bounded number of rows and columns across at most $n$ entries. For a processed interval $[a,b]$ of length $r$, every matrix write has a row or column in that interval. Before the chase, initialization or the preceding accepted check has established that the complementary below-subdiagonal positions are zero. The prescribed post-block check enumerates only the positions touching this interval: at most $nr$ in its rows and $nr$ in its columns, plus the $n$ row-loop visits. It is therefore equivalent to the full Hessenberg check and costs $O(nr)$, since every active interval has $r\ge2$. The disjoint active lengths sum to at most $n$, so all these checks cost $O(n^2)$ per sweep, even if every polynomial vector is zero and all factors are identities. The explicit partition/deflation passes and direct shift-table lookups also fit within $O(n^2)$; for $n=1$ there is no active sweep. Thus a full sweep, including all non-controller status work, costs $O(n^2)$. The linear sweep budgets therefore give $O(n^3)$ matrix work for the two Schur calls. The same bound covers materializing the recorded factor products: initialization contributes $O(n^3)$ and each bounded-support chase factor updates only a bounded number of dense-factor columns, at cost $O(n)$. [F1, F2, given, algebra]
 
 
 1.3 Each exact Schur-certificate matrix product, the transform $U^*CV$, and the final transform $UYV^*$ cost $O(n^3)$. The block substitution has at most $n^2$ block pairs; each pair sums at most $O(n)$ earlier blocks of bounded size and solves a system of dimension at most four, so all substitution work is $O(n^3)$. [F1, algebra]
